{"type":"cards","title":"Service credit thresholds and SLA tiers","lead":"Multi-zone Compute Engine deployments elevate baseline availability and carry contractual SLA credits.","context":"Compute Engine contractual uptime commitments and service credit tiers across single-zone and multi-zone SQL Server topologies.","items":[{"label":"Multi-zone SLA","value":"100%","title":"100% · Multi-zone SLA","text":"Scope: SQL Server instances deployed across Zone A and Zone B in Region 1 · Requirement: concurrent loss of connectivity across multiple zones to trigger SLA breach. Simultaneous outages ensure only wide failures qualify."},{"label":"Minimum credit","value":"10%","title":"10% · Minimum credit","text":"Trigger: monthly uptime falls below the contractual Compute Engine threshold · Application: financial credit applied directly against next monthly billing cycle for affected VMs. Adjustments appear on customer statements."},{"label":"Maximum credit","value":"50%","title":"50% · Maximum credit","text":"Trigger: sustained monthly uptime degradation below the lowest SLA bracket · Contrast: single-zone VMs carry a lower baseline commitment before credits apply. High availability multi-zone setups provide stronger terms."}],"takeaway":"Deploy synchronous Always On nodes across two zones in Region 1 to qualify for the 100% multi-zone Compute Engine SLA.","notes":"Google Cloud Compute Engine provides a Monthly Uptime Percentage of at least 99.99% for instances deployed across multiple zones in the same region, compared to a lower baseline SLA for single-zone instances. If monthly uptime drops below defined thresholds, Google Cloud provides financial service credits ranging from 10% to 50%.

